ƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarky-<p>A half-chicken half-hare, a skunk and a tortoise go in search of a 3,000-year-old groundhog in this wholesome animation with a surprisingly complex plot</p><p></p><p>Although this animated feature doesnâ€™t have a title as utterly awesome as its predecessor, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chickenhare_and_the_Hamster_of_Darkness">Chickenhare and the Hamster of Darkness</a>, itâ€™s still a perfectly adequate 90-ish minutes of creamy pastel-coloured picaresque entertainment. It feels like itâ€™s skewed in the direction of younger viewers given the simple character designs, general wholesomeness and easy-on-the-jokes tone, but the plot is surprisingly complicated. The lead character is named Chickenhare because of course he is half-chicken half-hare, although in practice he looks more like a hare than a chicken other than a tuft of feathers on the crown of his head. He is best friends with a skunk named Meg, equipped with the awesome power of stink and who is very butch-coded with her waistcoat, faux-hawk and tomboy characteristics; there is also a tortoise named Abe, who as you might imagine is less keen on running about than the other two.</p><p>After a pleasingly kinetic introductory action sequence that feels very Indiana Jones-inspired, during which the treasure-hunting trio run into a svelte but scratchy cat called Crolloq, an old school friend (girlfriend?) of Megâ€™s. Angry at Meg for assorted reasons, she leaves the other three in a pit but they eventually escape with the golden spork they were seeking all along, a doodah that proves useful later. One thing leads to another, like they do, and they end up on a new adventure in search of a 3,000-year-old groundhog with a backwards facing face who can turn back time. A new character accompanies them on the way: Gina, Chickenhareâ€™s long-lost half-sister who is, wait for it, a harechicken â€“ more aviform than mammalian.</p> <a href="https://www.theguardian.com/film/2026/jul/21/chickenhare-and-the-secret-of-the-groundhog-review-time-travel-fun-for-plucky-hybrid-critters">Continue reading...</a>ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;EfdGFh9aioadYdET3IfUuU0kFumDgOrP7odZ-RfAw_cƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkxaChickenhare and the Secret of the Groundhog review â€“ time-travel fun for plucky hybrid crittersƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;yOB3O9x5sZ5W0LUWJ6A7YQUhCvCgETyk9H_CszH0LuQƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkoSyndicationItemƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;f9YKORDSOw-CaH_Z9_BFKqB-nBEFq-WNSu3aWp2TCZcƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkxŠhttps://www.theguardian.com/film/2026/jul/21/chickenhare-and-the-secret-of-the-groundhog-review-time-travel-fun-for-plucky-hybrid-crittersƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;DYolkiJ4eV-v2n2f2gNJsYYQQWXE5FneM6gOjTKHCPMƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx]chickenhare-and-the-secret-of-the-groundhog-review-time-travel-fun-for-plucky-hybrid-crittersƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;QEfItXHiWlFcokoqP_JyMgtuXwe2FUepd1mawZXa7wYƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkwSyndicationSourceBundleƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;n4b-xZae_-VuOPhsX_lF7wyCOtYE3RjSsFdPI3HvmiYƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkgcultureƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;ouSVnyWfUxpC8dKtiBw4mZopZCrj8XtdkoU9gaXn_ysƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9ni:///sha-256;Qp_AeVKUaOo2et7tdbZBltCpO1gXtFjwr3u32eE6ZwAƒd